# React Native MVVM

Prefer MVVM for screens. Screen components are the **View**; custom hooks are the **ViewModel**.

## View (screen component)
- Render UI only — layout, styling, and wiring props/handlers from the view model
- Avoid business logic, API calls, validation, and side effects in the screen file
- Keep navigation setup (`useLayoutEffect`, header options) minimal; move reusable logic into the view model hook when it grows

## ViewModel (custom hook)
- One hook per screen, named `use<ScreenName>ViewModel` (e.g. `useSignUpViewModel` for `sign-up.tsx`)
- Colocate in `hooks/` (e.g. `hooks/useSignUpViewModel.ts`)
- Own state, derived data, mutations/queries, validation, and side effects
- Return a plain object: `{ state, actions, status }` (or similar) for the view to consume

## Model
- Data types live in `types/`
- API/data access lives in `api/` (React Query hooks, Supabase calls, etc.)
- ViewModels compose model-layer hooks; views do not call `api/` directly

## Example

```tsx
// hooks/useSignUpViewModel.ts
export function useSignUpViewModel() {
  const router = useRouter();
  const { mutate: signUp, isPending } = useSignUp();

  const signUpUser = (email: string, password: string) => { /* ... */ };
  const goToSignIn = () => router.back();

  return { isPending, signUpUser, goToSignIn };
}

// app/(auth)/sign-up.tsx — View only
export default function SignUpScreen() {
  const { isPending, signUpUser, goToSignIn } = useSignUpViewModel();
  // render only
}
```

## Existing patterns to follow
- `useFilteredPlaces` is a good view-model-style hook — extend this pattern to auth and profile screens
- Screens like `sign-up.tsx` and `sign-in.tsx` should extract inline logic into dedicated view model hooks
